# Keep shared spec data and fixtures as LF on every platform so the
# data-driven test suite parses identically on Windows checkouts.
*.tsv text eol=lf
test/spec/files/** text eol=lf
# The embedded copy of test/spec/signature.tsv (docs/design/
# SIGNATURES.0.md): byte-compared with it by go/sig_test.go, so it
# keeps the same line endings on every platform.
go/sigdecl.txt text eol=lf

# The same rule, for the same reason, over every other file the suite
# READS AS DATA rather than compiles: the documentation is executed
# (ts/test/docs.test.ts extracts fenced examples and runs them), and the
# published grammars are parsed to check their rule and builtin sets
# against the engine (ts/test/grammar.test.ts). Both broke on a CRLF
# checkout, and both broke QUIETLY -- the example extractor matched zero
# blocks and reported a documentation file with nothing in it, and the
# grammar's rule slice ran past a terminator it could no longer find,
# swallowing every later rule.
#
# docs/skill/ is covered here as well, though its reader happened to
# survive: examples.md fences carry no language tag, so the strip that
# would have been line-ending sensitive is a no-op there. Covered
# because that is a property of how the file is written today, not a
# property anyone maintains.
#
# This is belt to the readers' braces, not a substitute for them: the
# extractors also normalise line endings themselves, because a file can
# reach them from a source tarball or an editor rather than a checkout,
# and a test that only works under one git configuration is a test that
# does not work.
*.md text eol=lf
*.gbnf text eol=lf
*.lark text eol=lf
